## How changelog entries are written

Write this changelog for people who use `pave-init`. Start with user impact.
Put implementation details in an indented sub-bullet.

Start each top-level entry with one tag:

- New — a capability is available.
- Improved — existing behavior is more reliable or easier to use.
- Fixed — incorrect behavior now works.
- Changed — a default or contract changed.
- Updated — a dependency or runtime requirement changed.

Add each release at the top of `## Changelog`, newest first. Bump the `version:`
line and use the release date.

## Changelog

### 2.2.7 (2026-08-24)

- Fixed — shared-package generation now rejects malformed lead placeholders,
  quotes generated Markdown frontmatter safely, detects only real generated
  orphans across the full file, reports invalid UTF-8 without a traceback, and
  rolls back all outputs when an atomic replacement fails.
- Fixed — Codex now uses the native `$pave-init:pave-init` skill name and
  `pave-init:<role>` custom-agent names. The lead must run the live nested-agent
  preflight before Stage 1.
- Fixed — skill-quality validation now binds to the active harness:
  `skill-creator:skill-creator` for Claude Code and `$skill-creator` for Codex.
- Improved — CI parses generated Markdown frontmatter, validates binding schema
  values, checks placeholder and orphan edge cases, and tests atomic rollback.
- Known limit — the installed 2.2.7 plugin and namespaced roles load on Codex
  CLI 0.149.0, but the tested Bedrock runtime still rejects the first named V1
  spawn with `Invalid 'input': value did not match any expected variant`.

### 2.2.6 (2026-08-21)

Codex port follow-up — the native roles now preserve the Claude role model and
effort intent: `fable` and `opus` map to `gpt-5.6-sol`, `sonnet` maps to
`gpt-5.6-terra`, and each role keeps its approved effort. Codex V1 requires
`agents.max_depth = 2` for the reviewer and planner research delegates.

- Added a fail-closed release preflight that verifies the current generated
  source hash and the persisted root → reviewer → research-delegate thread
  chain. Model-written success prose cannot satisfy the gate.
- Added deterministic release stamping from this file to both plugin manifests,
  the marketplace entry, and the delivered README.
- Hardened shared-source generation: role templates reject every unresolved
  placeholder; normal builds refuse to overwrite a differing generated file;
  all 14 outputs carry `DO NOT EDIT`; CI checks drift, tests, stamps, and hooks.
- Restored doctrine lost during harness neutralization: concrete model mappings,
  reviewer continuity, explicit approval semantics, observing-first enforcement,
  generated-role and hook disclosure, stop-block cadence, and project-root
  discovery in the hook template.
- Codex CLI 0.149.0 still fails the first named V1 child request on the tested
  Bedrock runtime with `Invalid 'input': value did not match any expected
  variant`. The preflight records this as a release failure.

Improved — node planning and plan review now state what a decomposition
*is* before working with one: it rewrites the parent's definition of done
as an AND (all children) or OR (alternative children) of smaller
evidence-gated predicates, each settleable by world-produced evidence,
checked in both directions — a missing conjunct lets every child pass
while the parent fails; an unneeded child is inflation (§4.11).

- `agents/node-planner.md` step 5 opens with the framing (the planner's
  self-check while framing children); the completeness attack lands in
  `agents/pave-material-reviewer.md`'s "Is every element earned?" bullet
  (the independent detection): construct the run where every child
  succeeds while the parent's definition of done still fails.
- Placement was adversarially swept: 13 other candidate sites rejected
  as duplication under the skill's own doctrine (§4.7 reinjection at the
  binding moment, §4.11 smallest sufficient, §9.14.2 no always-on text
  for a rule one transition evaluates).
- A blind A/B review (planted-defect and clean fixtures, old vs new
  prompts) judged the change mixed → keep with revision. Planner half
  helps: the new-prompt plan covered a definition-of-done conjunct the
  old plan chartered no child to achieve, and was the less bloated of
  the pair. Reviewer detection was already at ceiling (both arms found
  the planted missing conjunct 2/2, sole BLOCKING). One probe-exhibited
  harm — a manufactured BLOCKING-shaped coverage finding on a clean
  plan — plus two doctrine gaps drove the two hardenings below.
- Hardenings: the entailment boundary is now one general rule — only
  the parent's definition of done decides what the entailment covers,
  and each clause needs a child that settles it, whether by build,
  disproof, or justified stop (§9.11); routing, ordering, and recovery
  that no clause names are machinery, and a gap there is a different
  finding. The reviewer's missing-conjunct attack requires quoting the
  unsettled clause and naming the short-stopping child — no findings
  built from actors the plan does not charter.
  - Replaces an earlier exception-shaped fix (special cases for
    exhaustion and disproof children); the general rule makes both
    cases fall out and stays scenario-free.
- Recorded, not done: a clean-fixture re-probe (4 reps per arm, scoring
  HIGH-severity inventions) — both arms currently over-fire on clean
  plans, a pre-existing calibration issue; and a PAVE 0.4 candidate
  paragraph for `references/pave-spec.md` §9.12 stating the entailment
  meaning of "child results integrate upward", a deliberate spec
  revision left for its own release.

### 2.2.5 (2026-08-19)

Fixed — the model/effort doctrine is consistent across planner, builder,
and assembly prose. A whole-skill consistency review found four conflicts
left by the 2.2.4 half-migration:

- The effort guidance no longer claims "unset means high": an unset
  `effort` inherits the session's effort — the same hazard as an unset
  model — so generated roles pin effort in frontmatter, with `high` as
  the default pin.
- Assignments are stated at the granularity the runtime can express:
  per-role frontmatter pins for lead-bound dispatches (the Agent tool
  has no per-dispatch effort override), per-`agent()` values inside
  workflow scripts. A role that needs two tiers on different nodes is
  two roles.
- The verify-retry escalation rule (a check that fails twice retries
  once one tier up) is scoped to workflow-script compilation, the only
  binding that can express it; the assembly step no longer states it as
  a graph-wide duty.
- The material reviewer no longer overrides `model` when dispatching
  research delegates (frontmatter pins are the authority), and the
  research delegate pins `effort: high` — every agent is now pinned
  except the deliberately-unpinned forward tester, as SKILL.md claims.

### 2.2.4 (2026-08-19)

Fixed — SKILL.md's Multi-agent contract no longer lists stale per-agent
model/effort pins or a per-dispatch planner escalation rule; both
contradicted the agent frontmatter, which has pinned the planner at the
top tier since the pins moved there. Pins now live in frontmatter alone,
with the forward tester deliberately unpinned so the clean-room test
predicts behavior at real session defaults.
  - `orchestration/explore-and-plan.md` dispatch and assembly steps
    updated to match: no per-dispatch override, and "the escalation
    rule" disambiguated to the builder's verify-retry rule.

New — the node planner now carries a Model and effort section for the
workflows it plans: always assign `model` (an unset model inherits the
lead's, silently running every worker on the session's top model and
inviting throttling), match the tier to the seat, and set `effort` only
when a level buys something, calibrated to Anthropic's published effort
guidance. Replaces the old "leave both unset" advice.

Improved — pave-spec §9.12 now states the mechanism behind the
one-agent test: an actor's judgment degrades as its context fills with
interleaved detail, volume alone does not force a split when evidence
can live in artifacts, and what forces one is a judgment needing too
many interacting concerns held at once.

### 2.2.3 (2026-08-19)

Changed — the adversarial-debate entry in `technique-selection.md` is
reframed from selection to filtration, matching the technique's original
intent: debate exists to disprove surface-viable candidates at arguing
cost instead of building cost, and whatever survives — several, one, or
none — advances. A no-survivor debate is the technique working.
  - Consider-when now keys on options that look viable at the surface
    with caveats findable before building; skip-when adds the
    straightforward-path case (nothing to filter) and the boundary that
    debate cannot test what only building reveals.

### 2.2.2 (2026-08-19)

Improved — `technique-selection.md` names two invariants generated
workflows previously had to rediscover on their own: an authored
mechanism, not a knob flip (config-only output satisfies checks without
doing the named work), and scripts own derived numbers (a deterministic
script computes every evidence-derived number and records its recompute
command in the artifact).
  - Found by a doctrine audit against the source system's intent map:
    both were absent from doctrine yet present in the first generated
    package — rediscovered by luck, now explicit.

Improved — two smaller hardenings in the same doc: the stage-audit recipe
now orders reconstruction before any checklist is read, and acceptance
standards (thresholds, rubrics, references) freeze before the attempt
they judge.

### 2.2.1 (2026-08-19)

Fixed — the planning-layout warning no longer fires on `root-contract.md`.
The root contract is a lead-owned planning file, but the hook's allowlist
did not name it, so every write to it drew a warning that a correct file
was misplaced. An audit of two real runs found 10 of 14 warnings were this
false positive, which trains the lead to ignore the 4 that were real.
  - `references/planning-layout.md` now lists `root-contract.md` in the
    layout tree and in the lead's row of the write-ownership table; the
    hook allows it and still warns when a subagent writes it, the same
    ownership check `frontier.yaml` already had.

Fixed — `build/` and `assembly-checklist.md` are declared run artifacts.
Both are lead working state that runs already produced, but no rule placed
them, so a reviewer reading `SKILL.md` alone would score them as stray.

Fixed — the final-skill review record is named `reviews/final-skill-review.md`
everywhere. `SKILL.md`, `references/pave-init.pave.yaml`, and
`references/pave-init-traceability.md` said `final-review.md` while runs
wrote `final-skill-review.md`, so a resume looking for the declared name
would find nothing and could conclude the gate never ran.

### 2.2.0 (2026-08-18)

New — `references/technique-selection.md`: a decision aid for choosing
review and supervision techniques (adversarial debate, advisory monitor,
stage audit by reconstruction, doer/tester split, one terminal metric,
rejection ledger, edge-triggered reminders). Each entry states when the
technique earns its cost, when it hurts, its price, and a minimal graph
