Improved — the stop nudge now also asks the lead to retire finished or
unneeded subagents and teammates: an idle agent costs tokens, and a late
message from one can derail the run.

### 2.1.0 (2026-08-18)

Changed — pave-init now delivers a Claude Code plugin, not a bare skill. The
package ships a `.claude-plugin/plugin.json` manifest, role contracts as
registered agents under `agents/` (the role contract becomes the agent's
system prompt, dispatched by `subagent_type: <plugin>:<role>`, so generated
briefs carry only task context), and the lead skill under `skills/<name>/` —
the same shape as pave-init itself. Install with `claude --plugin-dir
<package-root>` for a session, or a marketplace for permanence.
  - Validation adds a plugin structure check (`plugin-dev:plugin-validator`
    when available, hand-check recorded otherwise); the clean-room forward
    test loads the package via a fresh headless `claude -p --plugin-dir`
    session so registered agent types resolve; builders follow the
    `plugin-dev` `plugin-structure` and `agent-development` skills for
    manifest and agent file formats.

New — generated workflows now get a default recovery loop for failures the
graph has no edge for: retry once if transient, investigate until root cause
with a persisted investigation record, match fix process weight to how
well-understood the fix is, then re-prove by the world. Two honest exits:
replan when the plan itself is the cause, pause/blocked when investigation
is blocked (`pave-spec.md` §9.8).

Changed — repair loops may now be unbounded by operator choice: the bound is
a declared counter, or unlimited attempts backed by the persisted
investigation record plus a designed stop the operator controls
(`pave-spec.md` §9.8; planner and reviewer contracts updated to match).

New — the enforcement record now carries a per-node evidence-gameability
judgment: could the doer mint, narrow, or stale-date its own success
evidence? Harden-first ladder: unfakeable provenance, then a check the doer
does not run, then practical adversarial review only when hardening is
impossible or a false pass is severe. "Not gameable" is recorded too, so
the plan reviewer challenges every omission (`pave-spec.md` §9.14.1).

New — every document a human reviews or approves from (briefs, delivery
report, generated README) now carries one writing rule: simple, plain
English, as concise as the content allows — short sentences, common words,
terms defined at first use, point first and detail after
(`approval-briefs.md` §Write for the reader). Enforced as prose, not a
hook: the reference is re-read at every gate, and a PostToolUse hook can
only observe after the write, so a hook would be over-enforcement.

New — the plan brief and README open the workflow section with a small
at-a-glance diagram: the happy path as a few plain-language stage groupings
(explicitly not graph node ids), before the full faithful Mermaid, so the
reader holds the workflow's shape before the detail. Loops and recovery stay
in the full diagram. pave-init's own README already renders this section;
generated docs now must.

Improved — pave-init's own README now follows the Write-for-the-reader rule
itself: the planning and enforcement sections are rewritten in plain English
with short sentences, and terms like boundary, frontier, and adversarial
reviewer are defined at first use.

New — node identifiers get naming guidance (`pave-yaml.md` §6): verb-first
plain words a reader can decode without context, outcome codes that state
what happened, and no sequence numbers in identifiers — edges carry order
and the brief's at-a-glance diagram shows the sequence.

Fixed — the staleness-reminder hook was inert on Linux: it tried the
macOS `stat -f %m` form first, whose failure output on GNU coreutils
pollutes stdout and crashes the script under `set -u` before any reminder
fires. The GNU form now runs first (both platforms still covered); all 17
hook tests pass, previously 4 failed on Linux.

Changed — Stage 2 explorers now persist their own reports: each
`pave-init:system-explorer` writes its assigned `exploration/<lens>.md`
directly and returns only the path and a short summary, instead of the
report round-tripping through the lead's context to be saved. The lead
confirms the file exists on return and verifies claims by reading the
persisted file, never the summary.

- The staleness-reminder hook no longer names `exploration/<lens>.md` as a
  lead-persisted artifact, so it cannot prompt the lead to overwrite an
  explorer's report with a summary-derived copy.

Changed — the node planner opens every dispatch with an uncertainty ledger
(facts with sources, assumptions, open questions) and returns it with the
draft; the material reviewer starts claim identification from that ledger
and prioritizes thinnest-evidence claims (`agents/node-planner.md`,
`agents/pave-material-reviewer.md`).

