### 1.3.0 (2026-08-17)

One theme: the Stage 3 planning workspace — the only multi-writer,
session-crossing state in a run — gets the same machinery every other run
artifact already had: a schema, a validator, a layout reference, and a hook.
Driven by one recorded dry run (2026-08-15, the Llama-3.1-8B vLLM-to-CS-3
port goal) that surfaced eight process failures, four of them in the planning
files. Self-consistency was the tell: `agents/material-reviewer.md` already
named "session-crossing state with no machine-checkable schema" and "a
multi-writer artifact set with no layout reference" as findings — pave-init
now passes its own reviewer's check.

- New — planning files have a shape authority. `schemas/run-state.schema.json`
  gains `$defs.frontier` and `$defs.fragment`, strict throughout
  (`additionalProperties: false`) with explicit free-form `notes` homes where
  prose belongs. `scripts/validate_run_state.py --frontier <path>` validates
  the frontier and every dispatched entry's fragment, then applies four hand
  rules: unique draft path per dispatch, draft named past `pending`, no
  re-authored dispatched node (frozen fields are referenced, never copied),
  no planner-minted `c<N>` ids (node-local labels stay legal).
  - Absorbs three and a half of the eight dry-run failures: the
    zombie-session path collision, the conflict-id collision, and the
    frozen-field copy fully; staleness-on-resync gets machine support (the
    `stale` status and `dependencies` field) while the rule itself stays
    behavioral in `orchestration/explore-and-plan.md` §4.4.
  - Evidence for the gap: the dry run's own `frontier.yaml`, written with no
    schema, was not even parseable YAML, and its stale survey fragment fails
    the new checks on exactly the two defects the reviewer had found by hand.
  - Frontier mode fails closed without pyyaml + jsonschema, like the graph
    validators; plain run-state mode keeps its never-fail-closed contract.
- New — `references/planning-layout.md`: the `planning/` tree, write
  ownership per actor, which-file-wins precedence, and prohibited patterns.
  `orchestration/explore-and-plan.md` §4.2–4.3 and `agents/node-planner.md`
  now point at the schema and layout instead of restating field lists.
- New — `hooks/planning-layout-warn.sh` (PostToolUse on Write|Edit,
  observing): a non-blocking warning when a write lands under `planning/` at
  a disallowed path, a subagent writes `frontier.yaml`, or a draft mints a
  `c<N>` id. Third registered hook; the frontmatter description now
  discloses all three — nothing registers silently.
- Changed — a check guarding an outcome's only edge MUST declare
  `on_failure_route` (a declared node or control endpoint), and a terminal
  endpoint used as a route MUST declare `terminal_status`.
  `references/pave.schema.json`, `scripts/validate_pave.py`,
  `references/pave-yaml.md` §9 and §13. Sixteen checks in
  `references/pave-init.pave.yaml` gained routes; the rule mechanically
  catches the dry run's "designed stop with no destination" class (three
  instances by two authors).
  - Design-language halves, so designers write it right before the validator
    runs: `references/pave-spec.md` §5.4 (designed-stop paragraph), §11 Edge
    card (failure-route line) and Memory card (superseded-files and
